WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.458 --> 00:00:03.837
In 1991, Mount Pinatubo 
in the Philippines erupted

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:07.632
becoming the second largest volcanic eruption 
of the 20th century.

00:00:08.049 --> 00:00:10.510
Its ash plume shot up 
past the atmosphere

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:11.845
and into the stratosphere

00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:15.306
dispersing a layer of aerosol particles
around the whole world.

00:00:15.557 --> 00:00:18.018
Those particles formed 
a sort of solar umbrella

00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:20.228
that reflected sunlight 
away from the earth

00:00:20.228 --> 00:00:22.063
caused global temperatures to drop

00:00:22.063 --> 00:00:25.483
by about half a degree Celsius
for almost two years.

00:00:26.151 --> 00:00:28.153
It's not the first massive 
volcanic eruption

00:00:28.153 --> 00:00:29.863
to cool global temperatures.

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:30.989
El Chichón volcano

00:00:30.989 --> 00:00:33.825
lowered temperatures by 
a similar amount in 1982

00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:36.703
as did other major eruptions 
over the past century.

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:39.998
Today, scientists have been exploring

00:00:39.998 --> 00:00:41.791
whether we could replicate 
this phenomenon

00:00:41.791 --> 00:00:43.209
to fight global warming.

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:46.337
Using a technology called 
solar geoengineering.

00:00:46.463 --> 00:00:48.882
Supporters say it's a tool 
we need to seriously explore

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:50.550
as climate change worsens.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.010
Understanding more about these things

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.345
that could potentially 
reduce suffering.

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:55.805
That's worth a lot.

00:00:55.805 --> 00:00:58.850
But critics believe the technology's risks
outweigh its rewards

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:01.311
and shouldn't be a response 
to fighting global warming.

00:01:01.311 --> 00:01:03.354
Sometimes more technology 
is not always better.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.440
There are some things that 
have been invented

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.068
that a lot of people would wish 
had never been invented.

00:01:08.276 --> 00:01:09.527
So which one is it?

00:01:10.862 --> 00:01:12.155
To understand the benefits

00:01:12.155 --> 00:01:13.865
and risks of solar geoengineering,

00:01:13.865 --> 00:01:17.285
I spoke to several scientists, 
lawyers, indigenous leaders

00:01:17.285 --> 00:01:19.496
with strong opinions on both sides.

00:01:19.996 --> 00:01:23.208
With stakes as high as the survival 
of the human race on Earth...

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.544
should we be exploring 
solar geoengineering?

00:01:28.046 --> 00:01:30.298
There's one thing scientists 
all agree on:

00:01:30.298 --> 00:01:33.134
Solar geoengineering could 
cool down the planet.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.678
Reflecting sunlight away 
from the earth 

00:01:35.678 --> 00:01:38.223
stops heat from getting trapped 
in our atmosphere.

00:01:38.473 --> 00:01:41.309
We could do that by injecting aerosols 
into the stratosphere

00:01:41.309 --> 00:01:42.602
like volcanoes can do.

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:44.979
That's the most commonly 
researched approach.

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:48.066
Some scientists are also looking into 
brightening marine clouds

00:01:48.066 --> 00:01:49.734
so they're better at reflecting 
the sun.

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:51.444
And other potential methods

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:53.905
include reflective shields 
in outer space.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.659
These initiatives are still in different stages
of the research phase

00:01:57.659 --> 00:02:00.370
but in order for us to deploy 
these technologies

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:03.706
and get large scale cooling effects, 
we'd need to interfere

00:02:03.706 --> 00:02:06.918
with our complex climate 
on a massive scale.

00:02:08.336 --> 00:02:10.296
One of the main reasons 
some scientists 

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:11.840
support solar geoengineering

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.634
is that we're currently on 
a pretty dire path.

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:18.721
Our emissions, mainly carbon dioxide,
keep rising.

00:02:18.888 --> 00:02:21.641
There's really no question
we must cut emissions in the long run.

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:25.145
But even if you cut emissions 
to zero tomorrow...

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.772
that does not eliminate climate risk.

00:02:27.772 --> 00:02:30.275
It just means you stop the climate risk 
getting worse

00:02:30.275 --> 00:02:32.861
because the climate risk comes from 
accumulated emissions.

00:02:32.944 --> 00:02:35.405
That's University of Chicago 
professor David Keith.

00:02:35.405 --> 00:02:36.698
He explained to me that

00:02:36.698 --> 00:02:39.033
unless we think beyond 
emission reduction

00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:41.953
the climate change impacts
we're already experiencing

00:02:41.953 --> 00:02:43.913
will continue to worsen.

00:02:45.874 --> 00:02:48.251
A recent study showed that
extreme weather

00:02:48.251 --> 00:02:51.254
accounted for nearly 10% 
of all global deaths

00:02:51.254 --> 00:02:53.715
and shows an increase 
in heat related ones.

00:02:53.798 --> 00:02:55.008
To mitigate those effects

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:58.219
supporters argue that we need 
to explore climate processes

00:02:58.219 --> 00:03:00.930
use computer modeling, 
and develop technology

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:04.184
to figure out if and maybe how 
this technology

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:07.604
could safely be deployed globally 
along with other solutions.

00:03:08.313 --> 00:03:10.690
The thing is, messing with 
our global climate

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.734
is very complicated.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.029
The whole climate system 
is wired together.

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:19.991
People could, technologically speaking, 
create major...

00:03:20.491 --> 00:03:24.037
unpredictable changes in the way 
the climate system responds.

00:03:24.162 --> 00:03:26.831
Oxford Professor Raymond Pierre Humbert 
is referencing

00:03:26.831 --> 00:03:29.334
one of the big concerns 
about solar geoengineering.

00:03:29.417 --> 00:03:32.378
Adding new elements into 
our atmosphere or stratosphere

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:35.506
will change precipitation patterns 
across the world...

00:03:35.924 --> 00:03:38.051
potentially worsening the extreme weather

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:40.303
we're already experiencing 
due to climate change.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:42.764
Nearly everyone agrees 
on these risks.

00:03:43.014 --> 00:03:45.516
—And... 
—You can't actually answer

00:03:45.516 --> 00:03:49.229
the most significant questions about 
the climate system response.

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:51.522
Short of a full scale deployment.

00:03:51.522 --> 00:03:52.607
A full scale deployment

00:03:52.607 --> 00:03:55.235
would require unprecedented 
global cooperation

00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:58.112
not just in terms of scale, but 
in terms of time.

00:03:58.488 --> 00:04:00.823
But the carbon dioxide we have emitted

00:04:00.823 --> 00:04:02.408
since the industrial revolution

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:06.913
and we emit today will continue 
to have a warming effect

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:09.582
on climate for thousands of years 
from now

00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:11.209
even if we stop emitting.

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:15.421
But in contrast, the solar geoengineering 
techniques proposed

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:17.924
have a very short lifetime in the atmosphere.

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:20.051
To create a continued cooling effect

00:04:20.051 --> 00:04:22.762
we would need to deploy them 
constantly for centuries.

00:04:22.845 --> 00:04:26.683
That means that you're committed 
to having institutions and treaties

00:04:26.683 --> 00:04:29.143
that can be adhered to 
for thousands of years.

00:04:29.143 --> 00:04:32.480
And that's a really unprecedented

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.941
sort of burden to put on
future humanity.

00:04:35.024 --> 00:04:37.568
If there an international disagreement

00:04:37.568 --> 00:04:39.946
war, global depression, 
or something that forces 

00:04:39.946 --> 00:04:43.032
a sudden termination of 
solar geoengineering...

00:04:43.491 --> 00:04:45.952
then the world gets hit 
with this rapid warming.

00:04:46.202 --> 00:04:48.788
That rapid warming is called 
termination shock.

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:52.250
And the risk of it happening 
is one of the big reasons critics think

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:55.753
solar geoengineering is ungovernable 
and unsustainable.

00:04:56.546 --> 00:04:59.382
And so far, we have a pretty poor record

00:04:59.382 --> 00:05:01.509
of figuring out how to move 
things forward.

00:05:01.926 --> 00:05:05.096
In 2021, Sweden rejected a pioneering project

00:05:05.096 --> 00:05:07.390
to test solar geoengineering 
technology.

00:05:07.765 --> 00:05:10.268
Environmental and indigenous groups 
issued a letter

00:05:10.268 --> 00:05:12.103
highlighting “the potential for creating

00:05:12.103 --> 00:05:15.523
drastically uneven, unpredictable changes 
on climate

00:05:15.523 --> 00:05:17.400
weather and biodiversity.”

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.278
and “geopolitical tensions
it might awaken”.

00:05:20.278 --> 00:05:22.822
It would cause great changes...

00:05:23.239 --> 00:05:25.825
to our environment and 
to our ecosystem

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:28.411
and those precious ecosystems 
that we are...

00:05:29.203 --> 00:05:31.164
advocating so hard for.

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:33.416
To protect and safeguard 
and secure

00:05:33.416 --> 00:05:34.876
and strengthen.

00:05:34.876 --> 00:05:38.713
Opposition like this is one reason
that most solar geoengineering research

00:05:38.713 --> 00:05:41.924
is taking place in labs with 
models and simulations.

00:05:41.924 --> 00:05:44.635
Turning the debate about 
solar geoengineering

00:05:44.635 --> 00:05:47.472
into one about how far we should take 
the research.

00:05:49.057 --> 00:05:50.808
In January of 2022

00:05:50.808 --> 00:05:56.439
400 scientists issued a letter calling for 
a non-use agreement on solar geoengineering.

00:05:56.439 --> 00:05:58.983
It included five core commitments, 
among them

00:05:58.983 --> 00:06:01.486
no outdoor experiments, 
no public funding

00:06:01.486 --> 00:06:03.821
and no support from 
international institutions.

00:06:03.946 --> 00:06:06.616
Professor Pierre Humbert was one of the scientists 
who signed it.

00:06:07.158 --> 00:06:08.743
He explained that the signatories

00:06:08.743 --> 00:06:10.536
are okay with low risk research.

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:14.707
So research that's on fundamental 
climate processes

00:06:14.707 --> 00:06:18.336
that doesn't develop the technology 
that leads to deployment

00:06:18.336 --> 00:06:20.171
that can provide useful information.

00:06:20.213 --> 00:06:21.672
Computer simulations and modeling

00:06:21.672 --> 00:06:22.965
are midway through the spectrum.

00:06:22.965 --> 00:06:26.344
Critics of solar geoengineering worry that 
the more modeling we do

00:06:26.344 --> 00:06:28.638
the closer we'll get to full scale deployment.

00:06:28.638 --> 00:06:30.973
But they acknowledge that 
some simulations are useful

00:06:30.973 --> 00:06:33.726
and a key part of overall science 
we can't eliminate.

00:06:33.768 --> 00:06:35.937
We are pretty clear on the idea

00:06:35.937 --> 00:06:39.023
that research that was essentially
technology development

00:06:39.023 --> 00:06:41.651
that would lead to deployment 
was something

00:06:41.651 --> 00:06:44.737
that the signatories are opposed to.

00:06:45.029 --> 00:06:48.157
Lili Fuhr, who works at the Center
for Intenational Environmental Law 

00:06:48.157 --> 00:06:49.534
told me something similar.

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:51.619
Soon as we let the genie out of the bottle...

00:06:51.786 --> 00:06:54.956
and if we not only just 
theoretically research

00:06:54.956 --> 00:06:57.542
but completely develop 
a certain technology...

00:06:58.626 --> 00:07:00.878
it will most certainly get used.

00:07:00.878 --> 00:07:03.339
We've seen that with other technologies as well.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:06.717
About a year later, 
in February of 2023

00:07:06.717 --> 00:07:10.972
110 scientists, including Keith, 
issued a letter in support of

00:07:10.972 --> 00:07:12.849
more research into solar geoengineering.

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:15.810
They want a robust, international 
scientific assessment

00:07:16.018 --> 00:07:19.397
that includes experiments that could 
potentially advanced technology...

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:22.483
but stated that “while we fully support 
the research

00:07:22.483 --> 00:07:26.070
this does not mean we support the use 
of solar geoengineering tech.”

00:07:26.446 --> 00:07:28.739
We can't bind the hands of the future 
and force them

00:07:28.739 --> 00:07:30.575
never to use these technologies.

00:07:30.575 --> 00:07:32.368
So if we make a collective decision now

00:07:32.368 --> 00:07:35.204
not to do serious research that 
doesn't prevent people

00:07:35.204 --> 00:07:36.497
employing this technology in the future.

00:07:36.497 --> 00:07:39.041
It just means they will deploy them 
with less knowledge.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:41.836
I asked Sarah Doherty, who studies 
maring cloud brightening

00:07:41.836 --> 00:07:44.589
at the University of Washington 
about research like this.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:47.175
Doing the research right now 
really is important...

00:07:47.467 --> 00:07:49.594
and it's important to making...

00:07:50.178 --> 00:07:51.804
equitable decisions 
down the road.

00:07:51.804 --> 00:07:53.139
Because the cat's out of the bag.

00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:55.183
People know these options exist.

00:07:55.224 --> 00:07:56.476
Some people think we should 
be going out

00:07:56.476 --> 00:07:58.102
and doing them right now.

00:07:58.102 --> 00:08:00.521
So that tells you how likely it is people 
20 years from now

00:08:00.521 --> 00:08:02.732
are going to be saying, 
“Let's go do this.”

00:08:03.566 --> 00:08:06.736
A big concern both sides agree on 
is the negative impact

00:08:06.736 --> 00:08:09.739
solar geoengineering could have 
on decarbonization efforts...

00:08:10.281 --> 00:08:12.408
and that the tech might act 
as an excuse for 

00:08:12.408 --> 00:08:15.495
oil and gas companies to keep 
selling fossil fuels.

00:08:15.953 --> 00:08:19.415
To unleash the technology of 
solar geoengineering on the world...

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:21.876
at a time when we've just barely

00:08:21.876 --> 00:08:23.294
started to get people interested

00:08:23.294 --> 00:08:25.505
in doing the hard work 
of decarbonization...

00:08:25.755 --> 00:08:28.216
that will just inevitably 
increase the pressure

00:08:28.216 --> 00:08:29.967
to just take business as usual.

00:08:29.967 --> 00:08:34.931
I absolutely agree that a risk 
around these technologies...

00:08:35.181 --> 00:08:37.308
is that they will be exploited

00:08:37.308 --> 00:08:39.727
by people who want to 
preserve emissions.

00:08:40.102 --> 00:08:41.854
But agreeing on the issue 

00:08:41.854 --> 00:08:44.315
doesn't mean agreeing 
on the path forward.

00:08:44.315 --> 00:08:45.983
That is a political concern.

00:08:45.983 --> 00:08:49.570
In my view, it is not 
an ethical basis

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:51.697
for restricting research 
on technologies

00:08:51.697 --> 00:08:53.115
that are potentially risk reducing.

00:08:54.408 --> 00:08:56.536
So far, Mexico is the only country

00:08:56.536 --> 00:08:58.871
that's banned solar geoengineering experiments.

00:08:58.996 --> 00:09:00.873
While the US is currently developing 

00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:03.000
a solar geoengineering research plan.

00:09:03.584 --> 00:09:06.587
World organizations focused on climate 
are now starting to make

00:09:06.587 --> 00:09:08.422
assessments and recommendations.

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:11.384
I can't tell you which one 
is exactly right.

00:09:11.384 --> 00:09:13.135
What I can tell you is that

00:09:13.135 --> 00:09:15.054
each and every single person 
I spoke to

00:09:15.054 --> 00:09:18.516
on either side of the issue cares deeply
about our future on this planet.

00:09:18.975 --> 00:09:23.020
And maybe that tension between them 
is actually a good thing...

00:09:23.688 --> 00:09:26.440
because while it doesn't make
our path forward clear...

00:09:26.691 --> 00:09:29.026
it can make us more thoughtful
and rigourous

00:09:29.026 --> 00:09:31.404
about how to solve this giant mess

00:09:31.404 --> 00:09:32.947
we are currently in.

